Theorem RL-30: backend-neutral whole-function memory-access fact derivation

  Preconditions:
    - RL-30 governs a backend-neutral AIMS fact. The final realization owner
      combines IC-3 ParamContract facts, the final IC-5 EffectSummary, and
      realized-operation effects exactly once. VM, native, compiled WASM, JIT,
      and LLVM consume the frozen result without re-running AIMS analysis.
    - MemoryEffectInputs includes may_throw and may_write_inaccessible independently of
      may_read_inaccessible. Untyped calls, I/O, panic/TLS state, allocators,
      and other unclassified non-argument writes set this input conservatively.
    - The neutral MemoryAccessFact vocabulary is readOnly / readWrite.
      readOnly permits reads from both argument and inaccessible memory and
      proves only absence of writes; it is not a memory(none) claim.
    - LLVM's spelling is a separate projection corollary: readOnly projects to
      generic memory(read), while readWrite omits a restrictive attribute.
      The shipped subset never projects memory(none) or memory(argmem: read).
    - Signature ArcFunction x AimsStateMap -> ArcFunction per the canonical
      proof notation:264; discharged against the sec-01 Realization.proof
      obligation.
    - depends-on sec-06: IC-3 ParamContract + IC-5 EffectSummary; VF-2 (d)
      validates the neutral fact derivation. Backend spelling fidelity belongs
      to the backend verifier, not the AIMS fact definition.

  Soundness property:
    Forall final realized functions f with IC-5 EffectSummary, IC-3
    ParamContracts, and realized-operation effects:

      (P1) Neutral derivation: MemoryAccessFact(f) = readWrite IFF any proven
           write source is present (may_allocate OR may_deallocate OR may_share
           OR may_throw OR may_write_inaccessible OR any_arg_written);
           otherwise readOnly. `may_throw` fails closed because the current
           panic/unwind runtime may write TLS or diagnostic state.

      (P2) Fail-closed inaccessible effects: may_write_inaccessible implies
           readWrite. Therefore an untyped call, I/O write, or panic/TLS write
           cannot receive a read-only fact.

      (P3) Projection separation: LLVM's projection is a fidelity corollary
           over the frozen neutral fact. readOnly maps to generic memory(read);
           readWrite maps to omitted. No target spelling participates in P1.

      (P4) Restrictive-attribute negative witness: may_write_inaccessible
           forbids memory(none), memory(argmem: read), and generic
           memory(read).

    RL-30 exports a common neutral fact that every executable backend may
    project subject to its own ABI and lowering constraints.

  Proof obligation:
    Constructive discharge by the refinement engine via definitional rewriting
    of the neutral write-source disjunction, followed by a separate LLVM
    projection-fidelity check.

    Part (P1/P2) — neutral-fact grid:
      call_free_no_write_readonly -> readOnly
      inaccessible_read_stays_generic_readonly -> readOnly
      allocation_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      deallocation_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      sharing_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      argument_write_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      untyped_call_inaccessible_write_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      throwing_runtime_state_is_readwrite -> readWrite
      Coverage gate: 8 derivations.

    Part (P3/P4) — target corollary:
      readOnly -> LLVM generic memory(read)
      readWrite -> LLVM attribute omitted
      Negative-direction witness: may_write_inaccessible must produce
      readWrite + omitted, never a restrictive read/no-access spelling.

    Engines dispatched:
      refinement (PRIMARY —
        section_08::verify_rl30_neutral_memory_fact; neutral IC-5 + IC-3 +
        realized-operation refinement followed by separate LLVM fidelity;
        emits Fail on an under-approximating fact or projection)
      rc_counting (SECONDARY — gracious-accept per the coverage-manifest RL
        row)
      case_analysis (SECONDARY — gracious-accept per the coverage-manifest RL
        row)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_08 refinement engine discharges RL-30 via an 8-row backend-neutral memory-access grid over IC-5, IC-3, and realized-operation inaccessible writes, with may_throw and may_write_inaccessible forcing readWrite, then checks the separate LLVM fidelity corollary (readOnly -> generic memory(read), readWrite -> omitted) and rejects every restrictive attribute on an inaccessible writer
